WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.160
Astronomers have found evidence of a Jupiter mass exosatellite orbiting a brown dwarf in the CD-35-27-22 system. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 233, lunar meteorites that test how the Moon's crust formed, a NASA safety warning on land or refueling, and new work on solar flare ribbons follow, with a week in review before we close. Observations with the European Southern Observatory's very large telescope measured the companion through the radial velocity method.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:35.300
The CRIS-ES recorded small shifts in how the brown dwarf itself was moving, pulled by

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.720
that orbiting body, according to Science Daily.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.740
The brown dwarf exceeds 30 Jupiter masses.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.020
Lead author Kevin Hoy described the arrangement as super-weird compared with our own solar

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:46.480
system.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.960
Alice Zerlo noted that the satellite is a giant gaseous body circling a highly massive

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.300
companion several times the mass of Jupiter.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.620
The system centers on a star of roughly half the sun's mass, and the brown dwarf in

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.260
its satellite form a nested hierarchy unlike anything around the sun.

00:01:04.175 --> 00:01:08.595
The satellite is massive enough to count as a planet under some schemes, yet it does

00:01:08.595 --> 00:01:09.975
not orbit a star directly.

00:01:10.775 --> 00:01:16.095
Confirmation would open a new category of observations for giant satellites around substellar objects,

00:01:16.515 --> 00:01:20.315
and it would force updated labels for moons and planets sharing one system.

00:01:20.315 --> 00:01:28.758
Looner meteorites, only 835 of them verified, represent 0.7% of all meteorites that fall to Earth.

00:01:29.258 --> 00:01:34.638
Universe-Today reports. Apollo missions returned 382 kilograms of samples,

00:01:35.198 --> 00:01:41.798
while 3 Soviet Luna missions and China's Chang-E5 and Chang-E6 missions added roughly 4 kilograms combined.

00:01:42.478 --> 00:01:47.798
Most of those meteorites come from randomly scattered sites across the moon, including the far side,

00:01:47.798 --> 00:01:53.418
and, unlike the Apollo landings in or near the geochemically unusual prosolarum creep terrain.

00:01:54.118 --> 00:01:58.778
Researchers use the chemistry and ages of the rocks to test the lunar magma ocean idea,

00:01:59.278 --> 00:02:03.098
the claim that a global ocean of magma covered the moon early in its history.

00:02:03.578 --> 00:02:09.158
The first identified lunar meteorites were collected in Antarctica in 1979, and they reach

00:02:09.158 --> 00:02:14.218
Earth after years to tens of thousands of years in Earth orbit, or up to tens of millions

00:02:14.218 --> 00:02:19.618
of years in solar orbit. Randy Koratev of Washington University in St. Louis noted that

00:02:19.618 --> 00:02:25.258
Apollo sites were chosen without orbital mineralogical data, leaving five of six missions inside

00:02:25.258 --> 00:02:31.298
one unusual region. Catherine Joy of the University of Manchester emphasized that the meteorites supply

00:02:31.298 --> 00:02:36.638
pieces of ancient crusts that may come from the far side, allowing tests of whether near side and

00:02:36.638 --> 00:02:42.927
far side crusts form the same way. The aerospace safety advisory panel at the National Aeronautics

00:02:42.927 --> 00:02:48.527
and Space Administration, or NASA, warned that unproven in-space cryogenic propellant

00:02:48.527 --> 00:02:52.327
transfer poses acute technical risks for Artemis lunar landings.

00:02:52.847 --> 00:02:54.247
Sat News Publishers reports.

00:02:55.087 --> 00:02:59.647
Panel members cited planning estimates of approximately 22 starship tanker launches

00:02:59.647 --> 00:03:03.687
to fuel an orbital depot, though SpaceX has disputed that figure.

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:09.887
The panel plans to issue a formal recommendation urging NASA to reassess its landing mission architecture

00:03:09.887 --> 00:03:11.747
ahead of upcoming program updates.

00:03:11.747 --> 00:03:22.807
The warning arrives as NASA restructures Artemis with an Earth orbit test for Artemis III in mid-2027 and a deferred crew landing on Artemis IV in early 2028.

00:03:23.547 --> 00:03:31.567
Former astronaut Charlie Precourt highlighted that dozens of rapid cryogenic transfers introduce compounding failure modes into human lunar flights.

00:03:32.518 --> 00:03:39.818
NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman is scheduled to deliver an agency-wide Artemis update that must address the refueling bottleneck.

00:03:39.818 --> 00:03:44.618
Administrator Jared Isaacman confirmed that the current effort is adopting the same

00:03:44.618 --> 00:03:50.558
urgent approach that took NASA from Apollo 7's splashdown to Apollo 11 in 9 months, according

00:03:50.558 --> 00:03:51.598
to Fizz.org.

00:03:52.018 --> 00:03:56.838
The Earth orbit docking tests for Artemis III will require three rapid launches from Cape

00:03:56.838 --> 00:04:02.818
Canaveral so Orion can practice automated rendezvouss with both Blue Origins, Blue Moon, and Space

00:04:02.818 --> 00:04:04.878
X's Starship Human Landing System.

00:04:04.878 --> 00:04:10.058
A lunar base near the South Pole is expected to see its first land or attempt by year's

00:04:10.058 --> 00:04:10.318
end.

00:04:10.718 --> 00:04:14.858
With additional rovers and orbiters following, so the first crude landing site already

00:04:14.858 --> 00:04:16.738
contains surface mobility assets.

00:04:17.458 --> 00:04:22.298
Lighter sortie suits will be tested on the International Space Station next year to reduce complexity

00:04:22.298 --> 00:04:23.718
for initial moonwalks.

00:04:24.549 --> 00:04:31.789
Researchers applied a new model to an M6.5-class solar flare observed on June 22, 2015 by the

00:04:31.789 --> 00:04:34.249
interface region imaging spectrograph satellite.

00:04:34.249 --> 00:04:39.209
light. The model quantifies how flare ribbons' substructure evolves and links those changes

00:04:39.209 --> 00:04:44.269
to fragmentation in the reconnecting current sheet in the corona. Fizz.org reports.

00:04:44.829 --> 00:04:49.449
Flair ribbons form when high-energy particles slam back into the chromosphere along magnetic

00:04:49.449 --> 00:04:54.949
field lines. The team found that energy deposition occurs in irregular bursts rather than a smooth

00:04:54.949 --> 00:05:01.089
process, with ribbons breaking into fragments of varying sizes. Lead author Marcel Courchato

00:05:01.089 --> 00:05:06.149
albello noted the work provides the first experimental demonstration of a physical relationship

00:05:06.149 --> 00:05:10.489
between observed ribbon shapes and the structure of the energy conversion region.

00:05:10.909 --> 00:05:16.209
Co-author Maria Casachenko stated that simulations had long predicted ribbon boundary behavior,

00:05:16.729 --> 00:05:20.169
but lacked observational confirmation until this quantitative test.

00:05:21.157 --> 00:05:25.437
A single solar flare can release energy equivalent to the simultaneous detonation of

00:05:25.437 --> 00:05:30.517
millions of hydrogen bombs, yet the conversion of stored magnetic energy stays hidden behind

00:05:30.517 --> 00:05:35.837
the sun's bright surface. Ride a magnetic field line as plasma twists and shears it until

00:05:35.837 --> 00:05:40.737
the line can no longer hold the stress. Then it snaps, reconnects into a simpler shape,

00:05:41.337 --> 00:05:46.457
and dumps heat and accelerated particles. Those particles stream downward along newly formed

00:05:46.457 --> 00:05:51.597
field lines, and the fine structure in the ribbons they light records breakup happening far above

00:05:51.597 --> 00:05:57.097
in the corona. The quantitative model ties the spatial complexity of those ribbon boundaries directly

00:05:57.097 --> 00:06:02.397
to the breakup of the reconnecting current sheet, that turns the ribbons into a remote diagnostic

00:06:02.397 --> 00:06:07.517
of coronal physics that cannot be watched on the spot. One persistent mystery is why the

00:06:07.517 --> 00:06:12.657
fragmentation occurs at the particular scales in the data. The model reproduces the pattern,

00:06:12.937 --> 00:06:18.257
yet it leaves open which instability or turbulence process sets the fragment sizes that control how

00:06:18.257 --> 00:06:24.129
and where the sun's magnetic energy is released. Pleiades, the star cluster, reaches its highest

00:06:24.129 --> 00:06:29.349
point at midnight during the Halloween period, a timing once linked by ancient Celtic druids

00:06:29.349 --> 00:06:34.689
to Sam Wayne, when the boundary between living and dead was thought thinnest, space.com

00:06:34.689 --> 00:06:39.989
notes. Observers in the United States can locate the cluster by tracing upward from Orion's

00:06:39.989 --> 00:06:44.369
belt to the orange star Aldibar on, then looking less than 15 degrees higher.

00:06:45.009 --> 00:06:51.049
The demon star algal and perseus appears to wink every 2.867 days as one star eclipses

00:06:51.049 --> 00:06:55.629
the other, dimming by a factor of 3.3 for roughly 10 hours at minimum.

00:06:56.269 --> 00:07:01.409
The constellation Cetus, a sea monster from Greek myth, shines in the southeastern sky with

00:07:01.409 --> 00:07:03.409
its head formed by a circled of stars.

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:09.249
Around the full moon on October 26th, Bright rays from the craters Tyco, Copernicus, and

00:07:09.249 --> 00:07:12.949
Kepler create spider web-like patterns across the lunar surface.

00:07:13.489 --> 00:07:19.049
The tarot meteor shower, produced by debris from comet 2P Enky, offers a chance to spot

00:07:19.049 --> 00:07:23.269
bright, slow-moving fireballs streaking away from a radiant near-the-pliades.

